&lt;h2&gt;What is an AI visibility content gap, and how is it different from SEO gaps?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;An &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;AI visibility content gap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span&gt; is a relevant information need, represented by a prompt or group of prompts, for which a brand is absent from AI-generated answers, lacks supporting citations, or does not have content that adequately addresses the question.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Traditional &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/content-gap-analysis"&gt;content gap analysis&lt;/a&gt; typically compares a website’s keyword coverage and search performance with competing websites. It helps marketers identify topics and queries that competitors rank for, but their own websites do not. &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-seo"&gt;AI SEO&lt;/a&gt; uses artificial intelligence to support traditional SEO tasks, such as keyword research, content analysis, and optimization. Both approaches — traditional SEO and AI-assisted SEO — focus on improving a website’s performance in traditional search results.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI visibility analysis examines a different set of outcomes. A page can rank well in Google and still be absent from a ChatGPT, Gemini, or Perplexity response. And, to add to the complexity, an AI assistant may mention a brand without directing readers to the brand’s website at all. That difference makes it important to understand three types of AI visibility gaps:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For marketers, treat these categories as diagnostic rather than mutually exclusive. A page may cover the right topic but lack both a direct answer and sufficient evidence to support a citation. The objective is to identify what is missing from the answer ecosystem, not simply what is missing from a content calendar.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;AI Mentions vs. AI Citations&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An AI mention occurs when an answer names a brand, product, or company. An AI citation occurs when the answer provides a source reference or link supporting information in the response. For marketers, understanding these differences in outcomes makes it easier to identify content opportunities.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For example, an AI assistant answering “What are the best project management tools for agencies?” might recommend a particular platform but cite an independent review site for its pricing, integrations, or suitability. The brand has achieved mention visibility, but its own content is not necessarily being used as the supporting source.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A citation to the brand’s website provides a different signal. A citation to the brand’s website means the assistant has surfaced that website as a source for the answer. However, a citation alone does not establish that the assistant recommended the brand, that a reader clicked the link, or that the answer generated revenue.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pro tip:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; Record mentions and citations separately. A brand can be mentioned in an AI answer without citing a single piece of its own content as a source. That is a completely different situation with a completely different fix, and combining them into a single visibility metric can conceal whether the opportunity involves brand inclusion, source selection, or both.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;How to Identify Content Gaps for AI Visibility Step-by-Step&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An AI visibility content gap analysis follows four steps: collect relevant prompt data, classify mentions and citations, map findings to existing content, and prioritize gaps by business relevance and the work required to address them. The content gap analysis process begins by reviewing the answers AI platforms currently produce, rather than assuming which articles a company should publish. Here’s what to do.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;1. Pull prompt tracking data to see where the brand is missing.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Prompt tracking establishes where a brand appears in AI-generated answers and where relevant questions produce no brand mention or citation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Start with a defined set of prompts representing the questions prospective customers ask during discovery, evaluation, and purchase. Group prompts by topic and intent so that individual wording variations do not become separate content projects.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I use &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt; to identify groups of prompts related to the services I offer through Cassie Clark Marketing. HubSpot’s AEO tool tracks prompts across ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity, showing how a brand appears in answers alongside com...

&lt;p&gt;For most teams, the answer to “which prompts should I track?” is this: track a representative sample of branded, unbranded, and competitor prompts across buyer stages, personas, and priority markets. Don’t fall into the trap of tracking everything.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt; &lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Remember: buyers don’t query answer engines the way they query Google. A prompt often reads like a brief. Prompts often include details like team size, budget, location, and follow-up questions that all reshape the answer. Faced with that variation, marketers either track everything and drown in noise or fall back on branded queries that inflate visibility metrics.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This guide aims to help marketers build an effective prompt tracking set. The guide explains choosing and balancing prompts as well as scoring, maintaining, and turning visibility and citation findings into content decisions.&lt;/p&gt; 
