&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: What’s different at a glance?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Profound and Scrunch both track brand visibility across answer engines, but they optimize for different workflows. Here’s how they compare, with HubSpot AEO included as a CRM-connected reference point:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Workflow Takeaway&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Which platform fits depends on where your workflow stalls. Profound suits teams that want multi-engine monitoring plus automated drafting; Scrunch suits teams whose bottleneck is technical crawlability and developer handoffs; HubSpot AEO suits teams that want visibility, drafting, and publishing tied to their CRM in one place.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before you commit to a paid tool, run a free, one-time check with the &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader"&gt;AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Methodology: How I Compared Profound and Scrunch&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I wrote this comparison based on a mixture of personal testing of free trials, each vendor’s public documentation, dated pricing pages, and product materials, cross-referenced against verified 2025 to 2026 G2 reviews. All figures reflect plans available as of September 2026, with the source linked at each claim. I held Profound and Scrunch, plus HubSpot AEO as a third reference point, to the same criteria: engine coverage, prompt capacity, reporting depth, actionability, technical approach, team fit, and pricing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="background-color: #ffffff;"&gt;Implementation&lt;/span&gt; effort varies by site setup and CDN access, so treat setup estimates as directional. Answer-engine results are probabilistic. A single query proves little, so read visibility as a trend across repeated checks. Citation movement after a change shows correlation, not proof that the change caused it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Disclosure: HubSpot publishes this comparison and offers HubSpot AEO, one of the three tools reviewed. I assessed all three against the same criteria and note where Profound or Scrunch leads.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Profound vs. Scrunch: Which covers more AI engines?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Both Profound and Scrunch advertise wide reach, but the models you actually get depend on the plan you buy, so check the number attached to your tier.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Here is how supported engines break down by plan as of September 2026:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Profound no longer lists a paid self-serve plan for brands: its free Trial samples ChatGPT, Gemini, and Google AI Overviews for seven days, and coverage of up to nine engines is quote-only on Enterprise. Scrunch starts wider with four engines on its cheapest plan, but its full nine-engine lineup sits behind a custom Enterprise contract. HubSpot AEO tracks the same three engines on every plan: ChatGPT, Gemini, and &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/perplexity-the-ai-search-engine"&gt;Perplexity&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;More engines does not automatically mean better. Focus on the engines your target buyers use: Developers and researchers lean on Perplexity and Claude, enterprise IT buyers sit inside Microsoft Copilot, and mainstream buyers default to ChatGPT or Google’s AI results. Match coverage to your audience first, then treat total engine count as a tiebreaker.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before you trust a vendor’s engine claims, run three checks:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Confirm tier gating and add-on fees.&lt;/strong&gt; Confirm which models are included in your exact plan. Profound and Scrunch bundle engines into tier upgrades, so getting more models means moving up a plan, not buying them individually. Some tools (Peec AI, for example) sell per-engine add-ons.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Ask how each engine is queried.&lt;/strong&gt; APIs, live-interface browsing, and user panels return different results, especially by region.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Separate sources from citations.&lt;/strong&gt; Only URLs a model links ba...

&lt;p&gt;Building an AI agent is a multi-step process. Marketers need to define the agent’s task, connect approved data and tools, and set limits on its actions. Once they master the basics, anyone — marketers, sales reps, or customer teams — can build agents with ease.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Agent Builder" height="58" width="373" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Today, AI agent adoption is growing. A 2026 survey of 5,000 U.S. adults from Menlo Ventures found that &lt;a href="https://menlovc.com/perspective/2026-the-state-of-consumer-ai/"&gt;one in four AI users&lt;/a&gt; has used an agent, with 24% of respondents citing regular use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;To make the most of the tech, teams need to find the right use cases and start experimenting. This post covers how to build an AI agent for those just getting started. Discover how to deploy, test, improve, and set guardrails for your first AI agent.&lt;/p&gt; 
