&lt;h2&gt;What options exist for running AEO without a large team when deciding what to do now?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Answer Engine Optimization requires measurement, prompt tracking, and content updates. All three can start in a single week without a dedicated specialist.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Four foundational elements determine how well AEO runs on a lean team. Each one is covered below, along with how AI tools can handle the execution work without adding headcount.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;1. Conduct a baseline visibility check.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The baseline visibility check comes first. It establishes where the brand currently stands in AI-generated answers before any strategy work begins. A useful baseline covers three things: whether the brand is mentioned at all, how it is described when it appears, and which competitors show up in its place. Without this starting point, teams have no way to measure whether later efforts actually work.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Two affordable approaches exist for getting a baseline:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ol start="1"&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Manually testing a handful&lt;/strong&gt; of prompts across core AI search platforms like ChatGPT, Google AI Overviews, and Perplexity, then recording the results.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader/"&gt;Using AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt;, which returns a visibility snapshot in minutes without manual prompt testing across each platform.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ol&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI Search Grader is built for marketers who need a starting point without committing time or budget upfront. AI Search Grader requires no setup, no integration, and no dedicated tool spend. For a lean team, the value is speed — a marketer gets an initial read on brand visibility fast enough to fold into an existing workday, then uses that starting point to decide what comes next.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://blog.hubspot.com/hs-fs/hubfs/Frank%20H/what-options-exist-for-running-AEO-without-a-large-team-2-20261005-622358.webp?width=650&amp;amp;height=591&amp;amp;name=what-options-exist-for-running-AEO-without-a-large-team-2-20261005-622358.webp" width="650" height="591" alt="AI Search Grader dashboard showing brand metrics across ChatGPT, Perplexity, and Gemini with visibility scores and category breakdowns" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI Search Grader organizes brand performance into five categories.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sentiment:&lt;/strong&gt; The overall tone AI models use when describing the brand, evaluated across general tone, how that tone shifts across topics, and the credibility of sources shaping that characterization.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Presence Quality: &lt;/strong&gt;How substantively AI platforms discuss the brand, the quality of sources feeding that understanding, and the overall richness of available data.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Brand Recognition: &lt;/strong&gt;How widely the brand is recognized within AI training data.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Share of Voice: &lt;/strong&gt;How often the brand is mentioned relative to competitors within the same set of responses.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Market Position: &lt;/strong&gt;How the brand is positioned competitively compared to others in its category.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/ai-search-grader/"&gt;AI Search Grader&lt;/a&gt; gives marketers a one-time visibility snapshot. &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt; supports ongoing tracking, which makes AI Search Grader a useful starting point for teams not yet ready to commit to a full monitoring workflow. To get the most complete picture, combining AI Search Grader with a round of manual prompts covers both the structured score and the raw search behavior.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;2. Build a starter prompt set.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A starter prompt set should include 15 to 20 prompts, organized into four categories.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Branded prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Direct questions about the company by name, testing whether the brand shows up at all when someone asks about it specifically&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Category prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Broader questions about the product or service category, testing whether the brand appears when a buyer hasn’t named it yet&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Comparison prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Questions that pit the brand against named competitors, testing share of voice and how favorably the brand is positioned&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Objection-style prompts: &lt;/strong&gt;Que...

&lt;p&gt;When our blog team made the switch to an AI-first writing model in June 2025, my reaction was split. There was excitement on one side: I could see the inefficiencies in our process that AI might actually fix. On the other, anxiety. And while plenty of that anxiety was about the technology itself (how well these tools could actually write, how much we could trust them), my primary concern was my team.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;I’ve managed HubSpot’s blog writing team for three years, and before that was a HubSpot Blog writer myself. I understand what it means to have ownership over your process. Many of the writers I manage got into this work because they love writing. They enjoy the craft of finding the right sentence and earning a reader’s attention. Traffic numbers and conversions are secondary. During this AI transition, I felt the weight of what it could mean for those whose entire approach to their craft was changing fast.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Agent Builder" height="58" width="373" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So much of the public conversation about AI content gets the story wrong. There’s an assumption that because AI can write passably well, the hard part is over. Just apply the same low-effort polish you’d apply to an AI-drafted email or memo to your blog post, then ship it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;But I can tell you from experience: it doesn’t work that way. A blog post is meant to compete against dozens (or hundreds) of other posts on the same topic. In this game, generic, low-effort writing will not win, and that’s good news for your content team. It means that even though the writing process has changed, the craft remains important.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So, how do you balance craftsmanship with the efficiency gains AI promises? And how do you build a process that takes advantage of what AI is actually good at without falling into its traps? We spent the last year figuring that out. Here, I’ll share what we learned: where time was saved, where it got added, and what it takes to make AI work without compromising the quality standards you’ve spent years building.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#before-ai-writing-was-personal"&gt;Before AI, writing was personal.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-we-learned-from-letting-writers-lead"&gt;What We Learned From Letting Writers Lead&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#directly-comparing-pre-ai-vs-post-ai-writing"&gt;Comparing Pre-AI vs. Post-AI Writing&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#ai-content-at-scale-is-easy-ai-content-worth-reading-is-hard"&gt;AI content at scale is easy. AI content worth reading is hard.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-this-means-for-people-managers"&gt;What This Means for People Managers&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#my-takeaways-as-a-content-team-manager"&gt;My Takeaways as a Content Team Manager&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Before AI, writing was personal.&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Before AI, our writing process was, well, a writing process. While we had editorial standards and guardrails to ensure consistent quality, we didn’t enforce a specific method for how posts should be written. We hired writers and editors for their ability to craft, and we trusted them to use that ability. We understood that writing is more than just a technical skill. Giving our writers the space to develop their own individual workflows yielded content with personality and unique insights.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When training new writers, we taught a simple, flexible framework to help folks start to structure their time:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Outlining and planning&lt;/strong&gt; took around 30 minutes to an hour.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Research&lt;/strong&gt; could take one to two hours, depending on the complexity of the topic and the writer’s familiarity with it. This included sourcing quotes, finding original data, and writing from personal experience.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Drafting&lt;/strong&gt;, the part where someone actually puts words to the page, took around five to seven hours on average for a first draft.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Editing, QA, formatting&lt;/strong&gt;, and&lt;strong&gt; finalizing in our CMS&lt;/strong&gt; added another roughly two hours on top of that.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;For a standard blog post, you’re easily looking at a full day’s work, usually longer. The bulk of that time lived in the drafting phase — writing the words, finding the right structure, and figuring out how to explain something clearly was the core of the job.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What We Learned From Letting Writers Lead&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When we formally adopted AI-assisted writing in June 2025, my approach was deliberate: don’t over-prescribe. Allowing our writers to develop their own processes had served us well for over a decade, and we wanted to maintain that sense of control for our writing team as much as possible, even as we added AI to the mix.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We gave writers access to &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-tools-grow-blog"&gt;AI tools&lt;/a&gt; (specifically &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/claude-vs-chatgpt"&gt;Claude and ChatGPT&lt;/a&gt;, along with NotebookLM) and space to figure out how to work them into their process. Even with AI, I believed writing is personal, and I wasn’t going to tell a team of experienced writers exactly how to do their jobs. Our goal was experimentation first, structure later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What followed was not the clean efficiency win I expected. AI solved some problems and introduced new ones. But by working through those problems with our writers, we built a process that we know works.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Faster drafting doesn’t mean faster content.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;At first, the signs were good. Writers were putting words on the page faster than before, and I assumed that would translate directly into faster posts overall.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;It didn’t.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A couple of months in, after reviewing time tracking data and having conversations with my team, a more complicated picture emerged. The quantity of posts stayed about the same, but posts were getting longer, more detailed, and more demanding to produce. Writers were spending more time testing software, sourcing expert quotes, reviewing tools in-depth, and going deeper on topics. The time AI saved in drafting was being absorbed by everything else a competitive post requires.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;What made it harder was that some writers felt the pressure of both expectations at once: write faster and write more. When I understood that this was happening, I had to recalibrate. AI hadn’t decreased the workload. In many ways, it had increased the workload.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So my first lesson was: If you’re measuring AI’s impact on your team purely by post count, you’re going to be disappointed. The win is in what those posts become, not how fast they get there.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Research was our biggest surprise.&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I also assumed AI would make research faster. After all, instead of spending an hour getting up to speed on a topic, a writer c...
